$DIR/main.p:8:27: onPanic is the panic handler, so it must not wait
    A panic can happen anywhere: in an interrupt handler, or while the code that
    panicked holds a lock. The panic handler runs on top of that code, so if it
    waits for a lock or a channel, it may wait for code that can never run
    again, and the program hangs instead of reporting the panic.
    onPanic calls rt.CatchAllocFree (main.p:8), which calls a.Free
    (goexit.p:68), which may be (*waiting).Free, which calls rt.Park (main.p:5),
    which may wait.
    a.Free calls through an interface, so it counts as calling the Free method
    of every type the program converts to rt.Allocator.
    Report the panic with code that does not wait: write to the console or a
    device directly, then halt.
    A kernel that waits in an interrupt handler hangs, often only under load and
    far from the cause, so Plutonium checks the whole program at compile time
    instead.
    (see "Interrupts" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.10)
    (run "pluto explain E2584" for a longer explanation)

$PLUTOROOT/pluto/rt/sched_linux.p:431:37: rt.(*hostedSched).Wake is the scheduler's Wake, which interrupt handlers reach through rt.Ready, so it must not wait
    rt.Ready calls the scheduler's Wake, and interrupt handlers may call
    rt.Ready (or make a channel operation that does), so Wake runs under the
    same rule as a handler: it runs on top of whatever code the CPU was
    executing, and must not wait for anything that code may hold.
    rt.(*hostedSched).Wake calls hostedReady (sched_linux.p:431), which calls
    runqput (sched_linux.p:452), which calls rt.panicIndex (sched_linux.p:737),
    which calls fatal (rt.p:283), which calls raise (panic.p:169), which calls
    rt.strRelease (panic.p:190), which calls strFree (str.p:93), which calls
    a.Free (str.p:123), which may be (*waiting).Free, which calls rt.Park
    (main.p:5), which may wait.
    a.Free calls through an interface, so it counts as calling the Free method
    of every type the program converts to rt.Allocator.
    A kernel that waits in an interrupt handler hangs, often only under load and
    far from the cause, so Plutonium checks the whole program at compile time
    instead.
    To hand work to normal code, use a non-blocking send:
        select { case work <- item: default: }
    (see "Interrupts" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.10)
    (run "pluto explain E2584" for a longer explanation)
